.TH CPUD 8
.SH NAME
cpud \- policy-enforcing remote execution service
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cpud
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Cpud
is a CLI-only replacement for
.IR rstyxd (8)
for servers that do not need the draw device.
It is normally started by
.IR listen (1)
through
.BR svc/cpud ,
which authenticates the connection and runs
.B cpud
as the authenticated user.
.PP
.I Cpud
reads the command string exported by the client, checks the real
command against
.BR /lib/cpud.conf ,
logs the session, mounts the client namespace on
.BR /n/client ,
binds the client's
.B /dev
over the server's
.BR /dev ,
and runs the command.
.PP
The client is
.IR cpu (1)
or
.IR rcmd (1).
No draw, mouse or keyboard device is involved; sessions are terminal
and command sessions only.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.B /lib/cpud.conf
contains one directive per line:
.TP
.BI allow " command"
Allow
.I command
(a basename such as
.BR sh ,
.BR echo ,
or
.BR rcmd ).
If there is at least one allow line, anything not listed is denied.
.TP
.B allow *
Allow every command.
.TP
.BI deny " command"
Deny
.I command;
deny overrides allow.
.TP
.BI log " path"
Write session start/deny records to
.IR path .
The default is
.BR /tmp/cpud.log .
.PP
With no allow lines
.I cpud
allows every command, matching
.IR rstyxd (8)
behaviour.  A missing configuration file is treated as an empty file.
.PP
Allowing
.B sh
allows arbitrary commands inside the shell.  For a restricted service,
list only the commands users need and do not allow
.BR sh .
.SH AUTHENTICATION
.I Cpud
does not authenticate anyone itself.
It is run by
.IR listen (1)
after the connection has been authenticated, normally through
.B /lib/cpusession.sh
which is just
.BI "runas $user /dis/cpud.dis" .
.I Listen
sets
.B $user
to the name in the client's certificate, so the session runs as that
name and
.I cpud
records it in the log.
.PP
The certificates come from
.IR certify (8).
A service started with
.B listen \-A
skips authentication entirely: every caller becomes the listener's
user and may run anything the policy allows.
.PP
The user name is not a containment boundary inside a single Inferno
instance.
.B runas
changes
.BR /dev/user ,
but any process can do that.  What limits a session is the
.BR /lib/cpud.conf
allowlist and the resources mounted in the client namespace.
.SH FILES
.TF /tmp/cpud.log
.TP
.B /lib/cpud.conf
command policy and log path
.TP
.B /tmp/cpud.log
default session log
.TP
.B /n/client
client namespace mount point
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/cpud.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR cpu (1),
.IR rcmd (1),
.IR listen (1),
.IR rstyxd (8),
.IR svc (8)
.SH BUGS
Session counts and idle timeouts are not enforced yet.  Authentication
and channel encryption are handled by
.IR listen (1);
.I cpud
does not inspect the peer address or apply per-user policy beyond the
command allowlist, and a global allowlist means every authenticated
user may run every allowed command.
